1
00:00:01,630 --> 00:00:03,880
[Music]

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:10,919
The next day was rainy and dark. Rain fell on the roof of the barn and dripped

3
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:13,398
steadily from the eaves.

4
00:00:13,398 --> 00:00:18,919
Rain fell in the barnyard and ran in crooked courses down into the lane where

5
00:00:18,919 --> 00:00:21,960
thistles and pigeed grew.

6
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:26,599
Rain spattered against Mrs. the Zuckerman's kitchen windows and came

7
00:00:26,599 --> 00:00:32,359
gushing out of the down spouts. Rain fell on the backs of the sheep as they

8
00:00:32,359 --> 00:00:34,839
grazed in the meadow.

9
00:00:34,839 --> 00:00:39,960
When the sheep, tired of standing in the rain, they walked slowly up the lane and

10
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:43,480
into the fold.

11
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:47,319
Rain upset Wilbur's plans.

12
00:00:47,319 --> 00:00:53,000
Wilbur had planned to go out this day and dig a new hole in his yard. He had

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,000
other plans, too. His plans for the day went something like this. Breakfast at

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,200
6:30. Skim milk crusts, middlings, bits of donuts, wheat cakes with drops of

15
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:11,480
maple syrup sticking to them, potato skins, leftover custard pudding with

16
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:14,359
raisins, and bits of shredded wheat.

17
00:01:14,359 --> 00:01:18,440
Breakfast would be finished at 7:00.

18
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:23,398
From 7 to 8, Wilbur planned to have a talk with Templeton, the rat that lived

19
00:01:23,398 --> 00:01:28,839
under his trough. Talking to Templeton was not the most interesting occupation

20
00:01:28,839 --> 00:01:34,440
in the world, but it was better than nothing. From 8 to 9, Wilbur planned to

21
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:40,919
take a nap outdoors in the sun. From 9 to 11, he planned to dig a hole or

22
00:01:40,919 --> 00:01:46,919
trench and possibly find something good to eat buried in the dirt.

23
00:01:46,919 --> 00:01:53,000
From 11 to 12, he planned to stand still and watch flies on the boards, watch

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,440
bees in the clover, and watch swallows in the air. 12:00 lunchtime, middlings,

25
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:07,319
warm water, apple pairings, meat gravy, carrot scrapings, meat scraps, stale

26
00:02:07,319 --> 00:02:13,240
homonyy, and the wrapper off a package of cheese. Lunch would be over at 1:00.

27
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:17,240
From 1 to 2, Wilbur planned to sleep.

28
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:24,679
From 2 to 3, he planned to scratch itchy places by rubbing against the fence.

29
00:02:24,679 --> 00:02:30,200
From 3 to 4, he planned to stand perfectly still and think of what it was

30
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:33,880
like to be alive, and to wait for Fern.

31
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:40,038
At 4 would come supper. Skim milk, proender, leftover sandwich from Lurvy's

32
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:47,640
lunchbox, prune skins, a morsel of this, a bit of that, fried potatoes, marmalade

33
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:53,240
drippings, a little more of this, a little more of that, a piece of baked

34
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:56,839
apple, a scrap of upside down cake.

35
00:02:56,839 --> 00:03:02,759
Wilbur had gone to sleep thinking about these plans. He awoke at 6 and saw the

36
00:03:02,759 --> 00:03:07,960
rain, and it seemed as though he couldn't bear it.

37
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:13,319
"I get everything all beautifully planned out, and it has to go in rain,"

38
00:03:13,319 --> 00:03:20,119
he said. For a while he stood glooily indoors. "Then he walked to the door and

39
00:03:20,119 --> 00:03:27,878
looked out. Drops of rain struck his face. His yard was cold and wet. His

40
00:03:27,878 --> 00:03:30,599
trough had an inch of rainwater in it.

41
00:03:30,599 --> 00:03:37,159
Templeton was nowhere to be seen. Are you out there, Templeton? Called Wilbur.

42
00:03:37,159 --> 00:03:39,639
There was no answer.

43
00:03:39,639 --> 00:03:45,320
Suddenly, Wilbur felt lonely and friendless.

44
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:48,679
"One day just like another," he groaned.

45
00:03:48,679 --> 00:03:55,480
"I'm very young. I have no real friend here in the barn. It's going to rain all

46
00:03:55,480 --> 00:04:01,398
morning and all afternoon, and Fern won't come in such bad weather. Oh,

47
00:04:01,398 --> 00:04:09,719
honestly. And Wilbur was crying again for the second time in 2 days. At 6:30,

48
00:04:09,719 --> 00:04:12,440
Wilbur heard the banging of a pale.

49
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:19,000
Lurvy was standing outside in the rain, stirring up breakfast. "Come home, pig,"

50
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,519
said Lurvy. Wilbur did not budge. Lurvy dumped the slops, scraped the pail, and

51
00:04:26,519 --> 00:04:32,920
walked away. He noticed that something was wrong with the pig.

52
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:37,400
Wilbur didn't want food. He wanted love.

53
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:42,838
He wanted a friend, someone who would play with him. He mentioned this to the

54
00:04:42,838 --> 00:04:47,960
goose, who was sitting quietly in a corner of the sheepfold. "Will you come

55
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:54,358
over and play with me?" he asked. Sorry, Sunny. Sorry, said the goose. I'm

56
00:04:54,358 --> 00:05:00,760
sitting sitting on my eggs. Eight of them. Got to keep them toasty, oyst.

57
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:06,679
I have to stay right here. I'm no flippity ibity give it. I do not play

58
00:05:06,679 --> 00:05:11,320
when there are eggs to hatch. I'm expecting gosslings.

59
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:19,000
Well, I didn't think you were expecting woodpeckers, said Wilbur bitterly.

60
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,880
Wilbur next tried one of the lambs.

61
00:05:21,880 --> 00:05:27,480
"Will you please play with me?" he asked. "Certainly not," said the lamb.

62
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:35,000
"In the first place, I cannot get into your pen as I am not old enough to jump

63
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:43,079
over the fence." In the second place, I am not interested in pigs. Pigs mean

64
00:05:43,079 --> 00:05:47,159
less than nothing to me.

65
00:05:47,159 --> 00:05:50,358
What do you mean less than nothing?

66
00:05:50,358 --> 00:05:56,679
replied Wilbur. I don't think there is any such thing as less than nothing.

67
00:05:56,679 --> 00:06:03,239
Nothing is absolutely the limit of nothingness. It's the lowest you can go.

68
00:06:03,239 --> 00:06:05,880
It's the end of the line.

69
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:09,400
How can something be less than nothing?

70
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:14,358
If there were something that was less than nothing, then nothing would not be

71
00:06:14,358 --> 00:06:19,320
nothing. it would be something even though it's just a very little bit of

72
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:28,599
something. But if nothing is nothing, then nothing has nothing that is less than it is.

73
00:06:28,599 --> 00:06:37,960
Oh, be quiet, said the lamb. Go play by yourself. I don't play with pigs.

74
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:44,199
Sadly, Wilbur lay down and listened to the rain. Soon he saw the rat climbing

75
00:06:44,199 --> 00:06:49,079
down a slanting board that he used as a stairway. "Will you play with me,

76
00:06:49,079 --> 00:06:56,599
Templeton?" asked Wilbur. "Play," said Templeton, twirling his whiskers. "Play?

77
00:06:56,599 --> 00:06:59,480
I hardly know the meaning of the word."

78
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:09,519
"Well," said Wilbur, "it means to have fun, to frolic, to run and and skip and make merry."

79
00:07:09,719 --> 00:07:15,800
I never do those things. if I can avoid them, replied the rat sourly. I prefer

80
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:21,719
to spend my time eating, knowing, spying, and hiding. I am a glutton, but

81
00:07:21,719 --> 00:07:26,039
not a merry maker. Right now, I'm on my way to your trough to eat your

82
00:07:26,039 --> 00:07:31,079
breakfast, since you haven't got enough sense to eat it yourself." And Templeton

83
00:07:31,079 --> 00:07:36,440
the rat crept stealthily along the wall and disappeared into a private tunnel

84
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:42,280
that he had dug between the door and the trough in Wilbur's yard.

85
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:48,119
Templeton was a crafty rat, and he had things pretty much his own way. The

86
00:07:48,119 --> 00:07:53,400
tunnel was an example of his skill and cunning. The tunnel enabled him to get

87
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:58,199
from the barn to his hiding place under the pig trough without coming out into

88
00:07:58,199 --> 00:08:04,519
the open. He had tunnels and runways all over Mr. Zuckerman's farm, and could get

89
00:08:04,519 --> 00:08:08,280
from one place to another without being seen.

90
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:15,159
Usually he slept during the daytime and was abroad only after dark.

91
00:08:15,159 --> 00:08:20,599
Wilbur watched him disappear into his tunnel. In a moment he saw the rat's

92
00:08:20,599 --> 00:08:26,039
sharp nose poke out from underneath the wooden trough. Cautiously, Templeton

93
00:08:26,039 --> 00:08:31,400
pulled himself up over the edge of the trough. This was almost more than Wilbur

94
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:37,960
could stand on this dreary rainy day to see his breakfast being eaten by

95
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:43,400
somebody else. He knew Templeton was getting soaked out there in the pouring

96
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:48,679
rain, but even that didn't comfort him.

97
00:08:48,679 --> 00:08:58,199
Friendless, dejected, and hungry, he threw himself down in the manure and sobbed.

98
00:08:58,199 --> 00:09:01,239
Late that afternoon, Lurvy went to Mr.

99
00:09:01,239 --> 00:09:08,358
Zuckerman. I think there's uh something wrong with that pig of yours. He hasn't

100
00:09:08,358 --> 00:09:14,920
touched his food. Give him two spoonfuls of sulfur and a little molasses, said

101
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:20,280
Mr. Zuckerman. Wilbur couldn't believe what was happening to him when Lurvy

102
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:26,838
caught him and forced the medicine down his throat. This was certainly the worst

103
00:09:26,838 --> 00:09:34,838
day of his life. He didn't know whether he could endure the awful loneliness anymore.

104
00:09:34,838 --> 00:09:37,960
Darkness settled over everything.

105
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:43,079
Soon there were only shadows and the noises of the sheep chewing their cuds

106
00:09:43,079 --> 00:09:47,480
and occasionally the rattle of a cow chain up overhead.

107
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:53,559
You can imagine Wilbur's surprise when out of the darkness came a small voice

108
00:09:53,559 --> 00:10:00,679
he had never heard before. It sounded rather thin but pleasant. "Do you want a

109
00:10:00,679 --> 00:10:06,039
friend, Wilbur?" it said. "I'll be a friend to you."

110
00:10:06,039 --> 00:10:11,639
"I've watched you all day, and I like you." "But I can't see you," said

111
00:10:11,639 --> 00:10:18,440
Wilbur, jumping to his feet. Where are you and who are you? I'm right up here,

112
00:10:18,440 --> 00:10:25,440
said the voice. Go to sleep. You'll see me in the morning.

113
00:10:26,519 --> 00:10:28,000
Chapter 5.
